# Private-name denylist — template. Copy to `.private-names` and fill it in.
#
#   cp .private-names.example .private-names
#
# `.private-names` is gitignored and MUST NEVER be committed. That is the whole
# point: a list of the names you are keeping off the internet cannot itself go
# on the internet. This template is tracked; the real file is not.
#
# WHAT GOES IN IT
# ---------------
# The real first names (and surnames, and handles) of actual people connected
# to your campaign: your players, yourself, anyone who has ever sat at the
# table. Not character names — those are invented and may ship.
#
# Every layer in this scanner suite except this one protects the maintainer:
# emails, machine paths, commit identity. This one protects everybody else.
#
# WHAT IT DOES
# ------------
# Every tracked file is scanned for these names, case-insensitively, on word
# boundaries. `Bram` will not match `Bramble`; `Cole` will not match
# `Coledge`. A hit blocks:
#
#   - `node scripts/audit-current-tree.mjs`  (warns if this file is absent)
#   - `npm run site:build`                   (warns if this file is absent)
#   - `git push public …`                    (FAILS if this file is absent)
#
# The push gate refuses to run without a list, because "the file went missing"
# is never the intended state for the person doing the pushing.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
# -------------
# Version 1.3.0 shipped with 110 occurrences of seven real first names, in
# probe fixtures written from real sessions. The dialogue in those fixtures had
# been rewritten to be synthetic; the speaker labels had not. Seven scanner
# layers passed it, because a first name in a parenthetical is not a
# credential, not a path, and not an author field.
#
# FORMAT
# ------
# One entry per line. `#` starts a comment. Blank lines are ignored.
# Add nicknames and short forms separately — the match is exact, not fuzzy.

# Your players' real first names, one per line:
# Firstname
# Firstname

# Your own real name (the handle is fine; the name is not):
# Firstname

# Anyone else who has been at the table — former players count:
# Firstname
